From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Miha Kralj <miha.kralj@outlook.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 20:12:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] net: phy: sfp: tolerate known-bad EEPROM on generic OEM 10G
 DAC

Generic no-name "OEM" 10G passive DAC modules (vendor_pn
"SFP-H10GB-CU1M") ship with EEPROM content that is permanently wrong
in two independent ways, confirmed on hardware across repeated reads,
unbind/rebind cycles, and full power cycles (so this is baked into
the module's flash, not a flaky I2C read):

 1. The base structure checksum (cc_base) never matches, even though
    every identification byte (vendor name, part number, revision)
    decodes correctly and consistently.
 2. The phys_id byte reads as 0x28, which has no defined meaning in
    SFF-8024, instead of the expected SFF8024_ID_SFP. phys_ext_id is
    correct (SFP_PHYS_EXT_ID_SFP).

Since 8ae94629abaf ("net: sfp: consider fixed link modules using SFP")
sfp_sm_mod_probe() hard-fails module identification with -EINVAL on
checksum mismatch, and sfp_module_supported() separately rejects any
phys_id it doesn't recognize -- either alone is enough to keep the
link down. Two precedents already exist for exactly this class of
fix: the Cotsworks checksum quirk (dev_warn+continue instead of
dev_err+EINVAL) and the Ubiquiti U-Fiber Instant phys_id override
(match on vendor identity instead of the wrong ID byte). Apply both
patterns to this module.

On LS1046A this module is in daily use in eth3's SFP+ cage
(sfp-xfi0). NXP's vendor ASK1 kernel (6.12.49) tolerates both defects
and brings the link up fine; only the switch to a strict mainline
drivers/net/phy/sfp.c on the ASK2/dpaa1 kernel (6.18.x) exposed the
difference.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/net/phy/sfp.c | 24 +++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 23 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/phy/sfp.c b/drivers/net/phy/sfp.c
index 16f64abf6..b60f65329 100644
--- a/drivers/net/phy/sfp.c
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/sfp.c
@@ -332,6 +332,17 @@ static bool sfp_module_supported(const struct sfp_eeprom_id *id)
 	    !memcmp(id->base.vendor_pn, "UF-INSTANT      ", 16))
 		return true;
 
+	/* Generic no-name "OEM" 10G passive DAC (vendor_pn
+	 * "SFP-H10GB-CU1M") stores a garbage phys_id byte (observed 0x28,
+	 * no defined SFF-8024 meaning) alongside an otherwise-correct
+	 * phys_ext_id. Same class of bad-EEPROM-content issue as the
+	 * checksum quirk above; match on vendor identity instead.
+	 */
+	if (id->base.phys_ext_id == SFP_PHYS_EXT_ID_SFP &&
+	    !memcmp(id->base.vendor_name, "OEM             ", 16) &&
+	    !memcmp(id->base.vendor_pn, "SFP-H10GB-CU1M  ", 16))
+		return true;
+
 	return false;
 }
 
@@ -2324,6 +2335,7 @@ static int sfp_sm_mod_probe(struct sfp *sfp, bool report)
 	bool cotsworks_sfbg;
 	unsigned int mask;
 	bool cotsworks;
+	bool bad_checksum_oem_dac;
 	u8 check;
 	int ret;
 
@@ -2378,6 +2390,16 @@ static int sfp_sm_mod_probe(struct sfp *sfp, bool report)
 	cotsworks = !memcmp(id.base.vendor_name, "COTSWORKS       ", 16);
 	cotsworks_sfbg = !memcmp(id.base.vendor_pn, "SFBG", 4);
 
+	/* Generic no-name "OEM" 10G passive DAC modules (vendor_pn
+	 * "SFP-H10GB-CU1M") ship with a permanently incorrect base
+	 * structure checksum baked into their EEPROM at manufacturing
+	 * time. The identification bytes themselves (vendor/PN/rev) are
+	 * consistently valid on every read; only cc_base is wrong. Treat
+	 * exactly like the existing Cotsworks tolerance below.
+	 */
+	bad_checksum_oem_dac = !memcmp(id.base.vendor_name, "OEM             ", 16) &&
+			       !memcmp(id.base.vendor_pn, "SFP-H10GB-CU1M  ", 16);
+
 	/* Cotsworks SFF module EEPROM do not always have valid phys_id,
 	 * phys_ext_id, and connector bytes.  Rewrite SFF EEPROM bytes if
 	 * Cotsworks PN matches and bytes are not correct.
@@ -2391,7 +2413,7 @@ static int sfp_sm_mod_probe(struct sfp *sfp, bool report)
 	/* Validate the checksum over the base structure */
 	check = sfp_check(&id.base, sizeof(id.base) - 1);
 	if (check != id.base.cc_base) {
-		if (cotsworks) {
+		if (cotsworks || bad_checksum_oem_dac) {
 			dev_warn(sfp->dev,
 				 "EEPROM base structure checksum failure (0x%02x != 0x%02x)\n",
 				 check, id.base.cc_base);
